What changed and why:

- **`@Observable` replaces `ObservableObject` and `@Published`.** Views now track only the properties they read. Hold the model with `@State` instead of `@StateObject`.
- **No `@MainActor` annotation.** With Default Actor Isolation set to MainActor, the class is already main-actor isolated. Make sure Approachable Concurrency is also on.
- **No `DispatchQueue` calls.** `load()` is `async`, and `URLSession.data(from:)` does the I/O off the main actor. After the `await`, execution resumes on the main actor, so you can assign `items` and `isLoading` directly. The `self` captures are gone too.
- **`try!` is removed.** A failed load used to crash the app. Errors now land in `loadError`.
- **A guard against reentrancy.** `await` suspends, so `load()` can be called again mid-flight. The `isLoading` guard stops duplicate loads, and `defer` resets the flag on every exit path.
- **`.task` instead of an unstructured `Task { }`.** The load is cancelled when the view disappears. `URLSession` then throws `CancellationError` or `URLError.cancelled`, which lands in `loadError`. You may want to ignore cancellation there.

**If parsing is slow.** `Parser.parse` now runs on the main actor. Move it off only if Instruments shows a hang. In that case, make the parser `nonisolated` and add `@concurrent`:

```swift
nonisolated struct FeedParser {
  @concurrent
  func parse(_ data: Data) async -> [Item] { Parser.parse(data) }
}
```

Then call `items = await FeedParser().parse(data)`. `Item` must be a `Sendable` value type, and it should be `nonisolated` if the module defaults to the main actor. Don't use `Task.detached` or `DispatchQueue.global()` for this.

Under main-actor-by-default, the model can be a plain `@Observable` class on the main actor, with no `DispatchQueue` and no `@MainActor` annotation.

```swift
import Foundation
import Observation

@Observable
final class FeedModel {
  private(set) var items: [Item] = []
  private(set) var isLoading = false
  private(set) var loadError: (any Error)?

  func load() async {
    guard !isLoading else { return }          // re-entrancy guard: load() can now be called twice
    isLoading = true
    loadError = nil
    defer { isLoading = false }

    do {
      let (data, _) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: feedURL)
      items = Parser.parse(data)              // stays on the main actor
    } catch {
      loadError = error
    }
  }
}
```

In the view, use `@State private var model = FeedModel()` and call `.task { await model.load() }`. The task is cancelled automatically when the view goes away. If you need a pull-to-refresh, use `.refreshable { await model.load() }`.

**What changed and why**
- **`ObservableObject` and `@Published` became `@Observable`.** Views now track only the properties they read. `private(set)` keeps mutation inside the model.
- **`DispatchQueue.global()` and `DispatchQueue.main` are gone.** `URLSession.data(from:)` does the I/O off the main actor for you. When it returns, execution resumes on the main actor, so no hop back is needed and `self` isn't captured in an escaping closure.
- **`load()` is now `async`.** In Swift 6.2 an `async` function stays on the caller's actor, so it is main-actor here. Callers get cancellation and ordering for free.
- **`try!` became a `do`/`catch`.** A network or file failure no longer crashes the app. It lands in `loadError`, which the UI can show.
- **A guard against double loads.** Previously nothing prevented overlapping `load()` calls. `await` suspends, so a second call could start mid-load, and the guard stops that.
- **Cancellation.** If the task is cancelled, `loadError` will hold a cancellation error. Filter it out (`catch is CancellationError`, or check `URLError.cancelled`) if you don't want to show it.

**Only if profiling shows parsing hangs the UI**

`Parser.parse` now runs on the main actor. Don't move it off main until Instruments shows a hang. If it does, offload just that step with `@concurrent`:

```swift
nonisolated struct Parser {
  @concurrent
  static func parse(_ data: Data) async -> [Item] { /* ... */ }
}

nonisolated struct Item: Sendable { /* ... */ }   // value type, so it can cross actors
```

The call site then becomes `items = await Parser.parse(data)`. The `nonisolated` on `Parser` and `Item` is needed because main-actor-by-default would otherwise isolate them to main. Don't use `Task.detached` or `DispatchQueue.global()` for this.

